<worksheet>
    <title>Checkpoint 2</title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>[LT1] Slope fields analysis</li>
            <li>[LT2] Applications of differential equations</li>
            <li>[LT3] Separable equations </li>
            <li>[LT4] Integrating factor </li>
            <li>[LT5] Exact Equations </li>
            <li>[LT6] Euler's method</li>
            <li>[LT7] Modeling first order systems.</li>
            <li>[LT8] Phase portrait for linear systems with real eigenvalues.</li>
            <li>[LT9] Phase portrait for linear systems with complex eigenvalues.</li>
            <li>[LT10] Second order homogeneous differential equations</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    
    [LT1]
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                [LT1] Consider the population model
                                <md>
                                    \frac{dP}{dt}=0.3\left(1-\frac{P}{100}\right)\left(\frac{P}{20}-1\right)P,
                                </md>
                                where <m>P(t)</m> is the population at time <m>t</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               For which values of <m>P</m> is the population in equilibrium? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task >
        <statement >
            <p>
                For which values of <m>P</m> is the population increasing and decreasing?
                                Explain your answer in one sentence.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task >
        <statement >
            <p>
               For which values of <m>P</m> is the population graph concave upwards and
                               downwards?
                               Explain your answer in one sentence. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Sketch the rate vs solution curve, showing the equilibrium solutions and <m>y-</m>values where inflection points can occur. Roughly sketch a possible graph of a solution <m>P(t)</m>  where <m>P(0)</m> is in <alert>each</alert> region of you specified in the previous parts .
            </p> 
        </statement>
    </task>
    </exercise>
[LT2]
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            [LT12] Suppose that you have a water tank that holds 1000 gallons of water.
                        A briny solution <m>A</m>, which contains 30 grams of salt per gallon, enters
                        the tank at a rate of 30 gallons per minute.
                        Another briny solution <m>B</m>, which contains 50 grams of salt per gallon,
                        simultaneously enters the tank at the rate of 70 gallons per minute.
                        At the same time, the solution is well mixed, and water is pumped out of the
                        tank at the rate of 100 gallons per minute.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Since 100 gallons enters the tank every minute and 100 gallons leaves every minute, what can you conclude about the volume of water in the tank?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               How many grams of salt enters the tank every minute from both solutions? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>S(t)</m> denotes the number of grams of salt in the tank in minute <m>t</m>. How many grams are there in each gallon in minute <m>t</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Since water leave the tank at 100 gallons per minute, how many grams of salt leave the tank each minute?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write a differential equation that expresses the total rate of change in
                                <m>S</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify any equilibrium solutions and determine whether they are stable or
                                unstable.
                                [A Stable Equilibrium solution means that as <m>t</m> becomes large, <m>S(t)</m>
                                approaches that solution].
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                
                Suppose that there is initially no salt in the tank (i.e. <m>S(0)=0</m>). Find the amount of salt <m>S(t)</m> in minute <m>t</m>(i.e. solve the differential equation from part (e))
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What happens to <m>S(t)</m> after a very long time?
                               <m> \left(
                               \mbox{i.e. Find }\displaystyle{\lim_{t\to\infty}S(t)}\right)</m> 
            </p>
            <hint>
        <p>
            Hint: See page 32 in your textbook.
        </p>
    </hint>
        </statement>
        
    </task>
    
</exercise>

[LT3]
<exercise>
    <introduction>
       <p>[LT3] Solve the initial value problem.
                        
            
                     
                            <md>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dt}=2ty^2+3t^2y^2+2t+3t^2}</md>, and
                            <m>y(0)=-1</m>.</p>
                        
    </introduction>

</exercise>

[LT4]
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>[LT4] Solve the initial value problem.
                        
                            <md>\displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dt}=3t^2y+3te^{t^3}}</md>, and <m>y(0)=1</m>.
                        
        </p>
    </introduction>
</exercise>
  
  [LT5]
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            [LT5] Solve the initial value problem.
<md>
    \displaystyle{(2xy-\sin (x))+(x^2-\cos(y))\frac{dy}{dx}=0}
</md>
and <m>y(0)=1</m>
        </p>
    </introduction>

</exercise>

[LT6]
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            [LT7]
                            The spread of the disease gonorrhea in a sexually active heterosexual
population has been modeled with a nonlinear system of differential equations.
Consider a population consisting of 1000 females and 1000 males, and let
<m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m> denote the number of infected females and males, respectively,
at time <m>t</m>, which is measured in days. Let us make the following assumptions regarding the
rates of change of these two quantities.
        </p>
    

    <ul>
<li>
    <p>
        In the absence of infected males (i.e.
                                <m>y=0</m>), the number of infected females decays exponentially with parameter
                                (i.e.
                                <q>cure rate</q>)
                                <m>k_{1}=-\frac{1}{2}</m>.
                                In the presence of infected males (i.e.
                                <m>y \ne 0</m>), the rate of change of the number of infected females increases
                                by an amount directly proportional to the product of the number of infected
                                males and the number of uninfected females.
                                The corresponding constant of proportionality is given by <m>k_{2}=.0005</m>.
    </p>
</li>

<li>
    <p>
        In the absence of infected females (i.e.
                               <m>x=0</m>), the number of infected males decays exponentially with parameter
                               (i.e.
                               <q>cure rate</q>)
                               <m>k_{3}=-\frac{1}{4}</m>.
                               In the presence of infected females (i.e.
                               <m>x \ne 0</m>),, the rate of change of the number of infected males increases
                               by an amount directly proportional to the product of the number of infected
                               females and the number of uninfected males.
                               The corresponding constant of proportionality is given by <m>k_{4}=.0004</m>. 
    </p>
</li>
        </ul>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Set up a system of nonlinear differential equations based upon the preceding
assumptions. 
 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>[LT6]
               Assume there are currently 200 infected females and 100 infected males in the
                               population.
                               Use Euler's method with a step size of <m>\Delta t=.5</m> to estimate, to the
                               nearest tenth of a person, the numbers of infected females and males after 10
                               days. 
                               Attach a copy of your spread sheet including a graph of the solution. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the equilibrium values of the system.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Assuming there are currently 200 infected females and 100 infected males in the
                               population, what is likely to happen to the number of infected females and males
                               as time goes on, i.e.
                               as <m>t \to \infty</m>?
                               Support your answer with a graph. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>


  [LT8]
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            [LT8] Consider the linear system
                        <md>
                            \frac{d\vec{Y}}{dt}=A\vec{Y},
                        </md>
                        where <m>\displaystyle \vec{Y}(t)= \begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\ y(t)\end{bmatrix}</m>
                        and where <m>A</m> is some two by two matrix having eigenvectors
                        <m>\displaystyle \vec{v}_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}</m> and
                        <m>\displaystyle \vec{v}_{2}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                        The figure below illustrates the eigenvectors, <m>\vec{v}_{1}</m> and
                        <m>\vec{v}_{2}</m>.
                        Superimposed on this graph are phase portraits starting from two initial values,
                        <m>\vec{Y}_{1}</m> and <m>{\vec{Y}_{2}}</m>.
        </p>
  <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="Q7">
    <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,10,10)">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x(t)" ylabel="y(t)" />
    <group at="line">
    <line endpoints="((0,0), (2,1))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1" />
     <label anchor="(1,0.9)" clear-background="no">
      <m>\vec{v_1}</m>
    </label>
    </group>
    <group at="line">
    <line endpoints="((0,0), (1,2))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1" />
 <label anchor="(0.35,1.7)" clear-background="no">
      <m>\vec{v_2}</m>
    </label>
</group>
    <line endpoints="((3,6), (2,4))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1" />
    <line endpoints="((4,2), (6,3))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1" />

    
    <line endpoints="((0,0),(1,2))" stroke="black" dash="5" thickness="0.5" infinite="yes" annotate="no"/>
    <line endpoints="((0,0),(2,1))" stroke="black" dash="5" thickness="0.5" infinite="yes" annotate="no"/>
    
   
    
   
   
    <label anchor="(3,5.0)" clear-background="no">
      <m>\vec{Y_1}</m>
    </label>
    
    <label anchor="(4.7,3.0)" clear-background="no">
      <m>\vec{Y_2}</m>
    </label>

  </coordinates>

  <annotations
      text="The graph of the two eigenvector lines">
    <annotation ref="line-v1"
                text="The graph of the eigenvector v1 from (0,0) to (2,1)" sonify="yes">
      <annotation ref="line-v2"
                  text="The graph of the eigenvector v2 from (0,0) to (1,2)"
                  sonify="yes"/>
      </annotation>
  </annotations>
</diagram>
  </prefigure>
  <description> The graph shows the eigenvectors <m>\vec{v}_{1}</m> and <m>\vec{v}_{2}</m>  and the phase portraits starting from the initial values <m>\vec{Y}_{1}</m> and <m>\vec{Y}_{2}</m>  ."
    
  </description>
</image>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose you are told that the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are given by
                        <m>\lambda=5</m> and <m>\lambda=-2</m>.
                        Determine which eigenvalue corresponds to which eigenvector.
                        Briefly explain your reasoning.
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
          Write down the general solution <m>\vec{Y}(t)</m> of the system.
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
           Determine the formulas for the solution functions <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>
                       when the initial value is given by
                       <m>\vec{Y}(0)=\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 5\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                       <!--\vspace{2.5in}-->
                       
                          
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
           On the graph, sketch the phase portrait starting from the initial value
                       <m> \vec{Y}(0)=\begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 5\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                       Your curve should accurately convey the long-term behavior of the solution. 
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>[LT9] For the system
                       
                           <md alignment="align">
                               <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}&amp;=&amp;2x+2y</mrow>
                               <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}&amp;=&amp;-4x+6y</mrow>
                           </md>
                      
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
              Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and their corresponding eigenvectors.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Expand the general solution <m>\vec{Y}(t)</m> in the form
                                <md>
                                    \vec{Y}(t)=c_{1}\mbox{Re}\vec{Y(t)}+c_{2}\mbox{Im}\vec{Y(t)}.
                                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the solution with initial condition <m>\vec{Y}(0)=(1,1)</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Plot the phase portrait <m>x(t)</m> vs <m>y(t)</m> and also plot <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>. Classify the type of the solution.
 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>[LT10] Find the general solution for the following homogeneous equations.

                       
                      
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y^{\prime\prime}+5y^{\prime}+4y=0</m>, and <m>y(0)=1,y^{\prime}(0)=2</m>.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>y^{\prime\prime}+4y^{\prime}+4y=0</m>, and <m>y(0)=1,y^{\prime}(0)=2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>y^{\prime\prime}+4y^{\prime}+5y=0</m>, and <m>y(0)=1,y^{\prime}(0)=2</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>